@* A note on how a game was set up, as a small chip: the scenario when the adversaries took the
   match's title, a hand-built island, thematic maps, an extra board. Two surfaces say these
   things — the games list beside a match's title and the recorded game's setup card under its
   island — and each had a private fragment drawing the same .chip-sm with an optional glyph,
   under a comment promising the other one looked the same. One chip, so it does.

   The tone is one of the two setup tones in site.css: .chip-island (the default — most of these
   are about the island) or .chip-scenario. Layout stays with the caller, as it does for every
   chip: the games list drops these below sm, where the title is already sharing a row with the
   date, the spirits and the result. *@

<span class="chip chip-sm @Tone @Class" title="@Title">
    @if (Icon is not null)
    {
        <Icon Name="@Icon" Class="w-3 h-3"/>
    }
    @Label
</span>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The glyph before the word — "map" for an island, "book" for a scenario. Omit for
    /// a chip that is only the word.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Icon { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which of the two setup tones — <c>chip-island</c> (default) or <c>chip-scenario</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Tone { get; set; } = "chip-island";

    /// <summary>The tooltip, where the chip's word needs a sentence behind it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How the host lays the chip out — the games list's <c>hidden shrink-0 sm:inline-flex</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
